          <Link Id="VMC6RskyXaP4MHmTlyWNHd" Ids="HLT8lIYyf6M77MXq25MssQ,GjS7YfbGLD6lxbS0hLEXsE" />
          <Link Id="HWgofCL5lq9w3BEx26t6qK" Ids="BaTLTaKGiOkfrZ968ZoRgj,Mi1guccRLPisOeAzuUHhbg" />
          <Link Id="Da1kj1IzOiDMMoAYEGV5gl" Ids="Nn9tWUOOfGhuSXkO2KkbhS,QOq21mZkYLNhdLAJazlu9s" IsHidden="true" />
        </Patch>
      </Node>
    </Canvas>
    <!--

    ************************ Application ************************

-->
    <Node Name="Application" Bounds="100,100" Id="ASG31R38QriTWoRP2QULsV">
      <p:NodeReference>
        <Choice Kind="ContainerDefinition" Name="Process" />
        <FullNameCategoryReference ID="Primitive" />
      </p:NodeReference>
      <Patch Id="TlTmCOzd0pBflyDBWEoJLc">
        <Canvas Id="UcuClICYPmKICWf2nBlFCl" CanvasType="Group">
          <Pad Id="Mi2z6Nd17f19OqS99dBPny" Bounds="-820,-120,660,1900" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="Prompt - Grow a town.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;Two orange dots, 700 m apart as the crow flies, each standing on one 60 m stub of street. CLICK&#xD;&#xA;IN THE PICTURE (or press GROW). Every dead end pushes out one more 60 m of street - straight on, or round a corner - and&#xD;&#xA;now and then a side street branches off. Press it again. And again. FOUND stays False while the&#xD;&#xA;two little towns grow towards each other; on the eleventh press they touch, FOUND flips to True&#xD;&#xA;and a teal staircase of a route appears between the dots: 960 m. Keep pressing. The town keeps&#xD;&#xA;filling in, junction after junction - and the number does not move.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;That is the lesson, and it is not the one the design promised. Every street here runs north-south&#xD;&#xA;or east-west, so any route from A to B that never doubles back is exactly 600 + 360 = 960 m - the&#xD;&#xA;'Manhattan distance'. The crow's 700 m is unreachable on a grid, however many streets you add. A&#xD;&#xA;first route CAN be longer (a detour that doubles back costs 120 m, and a later junction removes&#xD;&#xA;it), but on this seed the first connection is already on the floor. A town that could grow&#xD;&#xA;diagonals would keep shrinking. This one cannot, and the number tells you so.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;WHAT THE RULE IS. A street is one 60 m segment on a 60 m grid. A dead end is a street end that no&#xD;&#xA;other street end touches. On GROW each dead end grows one child (a third of the time it turns&#xD;&#xA;left, a third right, a third straight on), and when the child went straight, four times in ten a&#xD;&#xA;second child branches off sideways. A child that lands on a grid point where a street already&#xD;&#xA;ends IS joined to it - that is the whole snapping rule, and it is why the grid matters: two&#xD;&#xA;streets that cross between grid points do not meet (BuildNetwork joins ENDPOINTS, not crossings,&#xD;&#xA;exactly as Tutorial 13 said - and on a 60 m grid of 60 m streets every crossing IS an endpoint).&#xD;&#xA;Nothing grows past the edge of the frame, and nothing grows past 200 streets. By the twentieth&#xD;&#xA;press or so every tip has run into something and the town holds at about 164 streets.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;The turns are not random. Each grid point hashes its own coordinates into a number between 0 and 1&#xD;&#xA;- the classic frac(sin(x*12.9898 + y*78.233)*43758.5453) that every shader programmer has typed -&#xD;&#xA;so the same tip turns the same way every run. Press RESET and GROW the same number of times: the&#xD;&#xA;same town (RESET is a right-click in the picture). Randomness you can reproduce is randomness you&#xD;&#xA;can debug. The same rule was run in a twenty-line Python script before this patch was opened; it&#xD;&#xA;said 'ninth press, 1080 then 960'. The patch says eleventh, and 960 at once. A model of a patch is&#xD;&#xA;not the patch - the fourth rung exists because the first three cannot see this.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;NETWORKS BUILT climbs by one per press, by design: the town really is a new list of streets each&#xD;&#xA;time, and BuildNetwork rebuilds. It reads 2 before you touch anything - an empty first frame, then&#xD;&#xA;the seed - so it is always presses + 2. Between presses it holds still, because the list is the same&#xD;&#xA;object frame after frame. Prompt Which door held it at 1 with a fixed town; this patch is the other&#xD;&#xA;half of the same contract - the counter reports change when there IS change. Watch what it does&#xD;&#xA;once nothing is left to grow.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;SAVE writes the current town to FILE PATH through Write GeoJSON, indented, one Feature per street.&#xD;&#xA;Open it in a text editor: LineStrings with coordinates like [-300, -180]. The file says nothing&#xD;&#xA;about what those numbers mean - RFC 7946 has no field for it, and this town is in metres on a flat&#xD;&#xA;canvas, not degrees on a sphere. Prompt Which door's file admits that with a 'units' property on&#xD;&#xA;every feature. This one does not; that is yours to add. Then point Which door's FILE PATH at it&#xD;&#xA;and see whether its reader accepts a town it did not write.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;WHAT THIS IS NOT. A toy, not a planner. Real growth models - space colonisation, tensor fields,&#xD;&#xA;Parish and Mueller's L-system city - grow along fields and constraints and never stop at 200.&#xD;&#xA;Streets here are straight, 60 m, axis-aligned, and join only at ends. The hash is not a random&#xD;&#xA;number generator, and the grid is doing most of the work the design called 'snapping'.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;THE MACHINERY, top to bottom - and this chapter is the first in the pack built from its own&#xD;&#xA;nodes, so the canvas stays short. Two seed streets -&gt; FrameDelay holds the town from one frame to&#xD;&#xA;the next -&gt; GROWTOWN, one node that is the rule: open it and you find a Cache region with&#xD;&#xA;StreetEnds (every street's two ends), DeadEnds (the ends nothing else touches), GrowTip twice&#xD;&#xA;(Branch off: the child; Branch on: the side street), PairsToStreets (back into LineStrings) and&#xD;&#xA;the Features for Write GeoJSON. Two Switch (Boolean) pick next frame's town (GROW pressed and&#xD;&#xA;under 200 -&gt; the grown one; RESET or an empty town -&gt; the seed) and hand it back to FrameDelay.&#xD;&#xA;BuildNetwork and ShortestPath read the held town every frame; DrawLines draws it, twice; Readouts&#xD;&#xA;paints the four numbers. Each node has a Summary - hover it.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;GIS WORDS. Network / graph, node (a street end), edge (a street), connectivity (FOUND), dead end&#xD;&#xA;(a degree-1 node), Manhattan distance (the grid's own metric), procedural generation. Snapping:&#xD;&#xA;moving a new point onto an existing one within a tolerance - here the grid IS the tolerance.&#xD;&#xA;Feature: one geometry plus its properties, which this file leaves empty.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;CREATIVE CONNECTION. L-systems, Conway, the Coding Train's space colonisation - anything where a&#xD;&#xA;few local rules produce a shape nobody drew. The GIS half is that the shape is then MEASURED: a&#xD;&#xA;route is a number, and the number falls as the structure gains loops - until the geometry's own&#xD;&#xA;floor stops it. Change the turn thirds, the branch odds, the frame - and watch the number.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;NEXT. Prompt Which door reads a town like this from a file - point it at the one you saved.&#xD;&#xA;Tutorial 13 is where BuildNetwork and ShortestPath first appeared, with one bridge to close.&#xD;&#xA;Tutorial 05 grew a shape; this grew a network. What would grow a surface?&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;Sources: none for the exercise (no desktop GIS has a frame loop to press). The hash is folklore&#xD;&#xA;attributed to no one; Parish and Mueller, 'Procedural Modeling of Cities', SIGGRAPH 2001, is cited&#xD;&#xA;and not implemented.">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">11</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
